TURBULENCE



Turbulence = the air is churning. Small pieces of air move up, down and
sideways in different directions, very close to each other.



 Smooth air:      → → → → → → → → → →        (all air moves the same way)
 Turbulence:      ↗ ↓ ← ↑ ↘ → ↙ ↑ ↓ ↗        (every piece moves differently)



The plane is shaken: bumps, drinks fly, people not in their seats get hurt.
Strong storm turbulence can damage the aircraft.




WHERE TURBULENCE COMES FROM

                    TURBULENCE causes
                          │
   ┌──────────────┬───────┴──────┬──────────────┬────────────────┐
 STORMS        RAIN SHOWERS    MOUNTAINS      JET STREAM       WAKE of
 (Cb)          (Cumulus)       (wind over     (clear air       other planes
 (worst)                       hills)         turbulence, CAT)
   │              │               │               │                │
 has rain      has rain        no rain         NO rain          no rain
 = radar       = radar         = radar         = radar          = radar
 CAN see it    CAN see it      CAN'T see       CAN'T see        CAN'T see


  ❗ The radar sees RAIN DROPS. No drops = no turbulence on the radar.
     Clear air turbulence (CAT) is INVISIBLE to every weather radar.




HOW A REAL RADAR DETECTS IT (Doppler)

  The radar measures how fast each group of drops moves TOWARDS or AWAY.


   Smooth rain:                              Turbulent rain:

   drops in one spot all move               drops in one spot move at
   at about the SAME speed                  very DIFFERENT speeds

     • → 10 m/s                                • → 18 m/s
     • → 11 m/s                                • ← 4 m/s
     • → 10 m/s                                • → 12 m/s
     • → 9 m/s                                 • ← 2 m/s

   spread (spectrum width) ≈ 1 m/s          spread ≈ 8 m/s
   → NOT turbulence                         → TURBULENCE (5 m/s or more)


                      Real radar turbulence (WX+T / TURB)
                                     │
        ├─ spread of drop speeds 5 m/s or more?  ── no ──► not shown
        │        yes
        ├─ within 40 NM?                          ── no ──► not shown
        │        yes                                     (too far, not exact)
        ├─ enough rain to measure?                ── no ──► not shown
        │        yes
        ▼
   MAGENTA on the screen (on top of green/yellow/red)

   - The GAIN knob does NOT change it (it measures speed, not strength).
   - Turbulence can be magenta even inside GREEN rain.
   - Some newer radars also scale the alert to the aircraft (weight,
     height) to say how hard it will actually shake.




WHAT MY APP DOES

 FlightGear
        │  gives: weather volumes with "turbulence" 0..1
        │         (small storm 0.6, medium 0.8, big 1.0)
        │  the SAME value really shakes the FlightGear aircraft
        ▼
 YOUR APP (weather.py)
        │
        ├─ Turn it into drop speed spread:  turbulence 1.0 = 10 m/s
        │       0.6 → 6 m/s     0.8 → 8 m/s     1.0 → 10 m/s
        │
        ├─ FLAT mode: the whole volume has that value
        │
        ├─ REALISTIC mode (more like a real storm):
        │     only REAL STORMS (Cumulonimbus clouds), not wide gentle rain ✅
        │     strongest where the clouds are packed (the middle)
        │     extra on the WALLS of strong cores
        │     rougher higher up in the storm
        │     patchy, and changes a little every sweep (air churns) ✅
        │     never stronger than FlightGear's own value ✅
        ▼
 YOUR APP (radar.py scan) = copy of a real Doppler radar
        │
        ├─ mode WX+T?                              ── no ──► not shown
        ├─ spread 5 m/s or more?                  ── no ──► not shown
        ├─ within 40 NM? ✅                        ── no ──► not shown
        ├─ rain/snow echo there (at CAL gain)? ✅  ── no ──► not shown
        ▼
   MAGENTA      (gain knob does not change it ✅)


  Simulated mode: storms in the scenarios carry their own turbulence value.

  Turbulence is also used by the WINDSHEAR simple rule
  (heavy rain + turbulence 0.6 or more, low down).




EXAMPLES


  FlightGear medium storm, turbulence 0.8, 25 NM ahead, mode WX+T

     FLAT mode:       8 m/s everywhere in the volume  ≥ 5  → whole storm MAGENTA

     REALISTIC mode:
         ┌──────────────────────────────┐
         │  edge of storm               │    edge:  8 x 0.35 = 2.8 m/s → not shown
         │     ┌────────────────┐       │
         │     │   packed core  │       │    core:  up to 8 m/s       → MAGENTA ✅
         │     │    MAGENTA     │       │
         │     └────────────────┘       │    low down (x 0.7): 5.6    → MAGENTA ✅
         └──────────────────────────────┘
       → magenta only in the core, like a real storm ✅


  Same storm but 60 NM ahead           → NOT shown (beyond 40 NM)
  Same storm, gain turned to -10       → magenta stays ✅
  Mode WX (not WX+T)                   → NOT shown
  Thin rain volume, turbulence 0.3     → 3 m/s < 5 → NOT shown




IS IT CORRECT?

  Doppler spread 5 m/s threshold               ✅
  Only within 40 NM                            ✅
  Needs rain (no drops = nothing)              ✅
  Gain does not change it                      ✅
  Magenta, only in WX+T                        ✅
  Strongest in storm core, patchy (Realistic)  ✅
  Clear air turbulence invisible               ✅ (no rain = not shown)
  Turbulence scaled to the aircraft            ❌ (some newer radars do)
  Real drop speeds                             ⚠️ made from FlightGear's 0..1
                                                  value, not real Doppler




WHAT COULD BE IMPROVED

  T1. Scale to the aircraft: a heavy plane high up shakes less than a light
      one low down (newer radars do this).
  T2. Show the spread in m/s on hover / in a debug line, to learn the numbers.
  T3. Lesson: put clear air turbulence (no rain) in Simulated mode → the radar
      shows NOTHING, but FlightGear's aircraft still shakes.




